  "response": "I ran `crimes context src/billing.ts` and read the file. It's rated **HIGH** risk with 11 findings (3 high, 6 medium, 2 low). Here's what I'd change, in order.\n\n## 1. Split `calculateRefundPreview` and enforce the refund rule in code\n**Findings:** crime_00012 (False Identity), crime_00013 (Logic in the Alibi), crime_00028 (Commented-Out Corpse). All three sit in `src/billing.ts:33-44`.\n\nThis is the biggest real-world risk in the file, even though its scores are lower than the other two:\n- The name says \"preview\", but the function calls `saveRefundAudit` and `sendRefundEmail`. If a UI calls it just to show a refund amount, it writes an audit record and emails the customer.\n- The rule \"Only owners can refund enterprise plans unless billing support approves\" exists only as a comment on line 39. The code never checks it, so anyone can trigger this path for an enterprise invoice.\n- The commented-out `legacyRefund` block on lines 33–37 sits right above it and suggests an older approve-and-send flow. Someone could copy it back in by mistake.\n\n**Change:** Make `previewRefund(invoice)` pure. Add a separate `issueRefund(user, invoice, approval)` that does the side effects behind a named guard (`canRefund(user, plan, approval)`), with a test for that guard. Delete the commented-out block; git history keeps it. First check who calls the current function, as the context guidance says, since some may rely on the email being sent.\n\n## 2. Use one definition each for `User`, `Invoice` and the plan type\n**Findings:** crime_00026 (Invoice contract drift, severity 0.96), crime_00010 (User drift), crime_00004 (`\"enterprise\"` repeated in 4 files).\n\n- `src/api/state.ts` has its own `Invoice` that is missing 6 fields from the billing version. Those include the money fields (`subtotalCents`, `taxCents`, `discountCents`, `currency`), `userId` and `createdAt`.\n- Its `User` is missing `createdAt`.\n- Nothing in the build checks the two versions against each other, so a change to billing's money fields won't reach the API layer.\n- The plan union (`\"free\" | \"pro\" | \"team\" | \"enterprise\"`) is written out inline on `User` (`billing.ts:7`). `src/api/plan.ts`, `src/jobs/plan-sync.ts` and `src/ui/pricing.ts` each repeat the `\"enterprise\"` string.\n\n**Change:** Export `User`, `Invoice` and a `Plan` type from one module. Have `api/state.ts` use them directly or through a named `Pick<>`. Decide on purpose whether the API should include the money fields. Do this before item 3, so the extracted helpers are written against the shared types.\n\n## 3. Write real tests, then break up `generateInvoice`\n**Findings:** crime_00033 (God Function, 204 lines), crime_00032 (swallowed error), crime_00029 (7 direct clock reads), crime_00039 (TODO, FIXME, HACK and XXX markers), crime_00025 and crime_00030 (weak tests), crime_00036 (God File).\n\n- **Do the tests first.** `test_gap: 0` makes the file look covered, but it isn't. \"renders the enterprise plan\" has no assertions, and \"creates an invoice\" only uses weak matchers. Refactoring a 204-line function with no real tests isn't safe. Write tests that check exact totals, tax and discount values for a few countries and plans, using a fixed clock and a fixed ID generator.\n- **Then pull out these pieces:**\n  - **Pricing and tax as one pure function.** The file's own comment says `tax()` repeats the tax logic inside `generateInvoice`, so merging them removes a second copy that can drift.\n  - **The audit log.** Per the FIXME, it currently writes the user's email and country to stdout, so it should stop logging that personal data.\n  - **Email building and sending,** moved out of invoice generation. The current retry loop catches errors and discards them, so the caller can't tell the email failed. It should return or throw the failure instead.\n- **Inject a clock** instead of calling `Date.now()` / `new Date()` directly. One correction to the scan here: crime_00029 says none of the 7 clock reads in this file decide a branch. But `recommendUpgrade` (around line 273) uses `Date.now() - user.createdAt` to pick between upgrade suggestions, so its behaviour depends on the current time and can't be tested without a fixed clock.\n- Splitting the 310-line file (crime_00036) should be its own follow-up change, as the context guidance recommends.\n\n**I left out:** the date, nav and asset findings elsewhere in the repo. They don't touch `billing.ts`. The scan also notes one suppression entry for `billing.ts` that is stale and no longer silences anything; `crimes audit-suppressions` is the command to review it.",
